+## Work Plan
+
+- [ ] Create `dedup.py` module with two functions: `dedup_embedding(texts, embeddings, threshold)` using dot product on the pre-computed normalized embedding vectors, and `dedup_ngram(texts, threshold)` using sklearn TfidfVectorizer with `analyzer='char'`, `ngram_range=(3,3)` and sparse dot product as overlap score. Both return a list of indices to keep. Do not hardcode thresholds — they are parameters to tune.
+- [ ] Write tests in `tests/test_dedup.py` for both methods: identical texts, near-duplicates, diverse texts, threshold edge cases. Test that both methods agree on obvious duplicates and keep obvious non-duplicates.
+- [ ] Create `evaluate_dedup.py` that evaluates both methods against `fixtures/golden_duplicates.json` using pairwise evaluation: for each threshold, compute all pairwise similarities across the 566 records, predict duplicate pairs (similarity >= threshold), then compare predicted pairs against golden pairs (all pairs within each golden cluster). Report precision (what fraction of predicted pairs are true duplicates), recall (what fraction of true duplicate pairs were predicted), and F1. Sweep thresholds to find optimal per method.
+- [ ] Add `--dedup-method` argument to `train_command()` accepting "embedding", "ngram", or "none". Use the optimal thresholds found above. Wire the chosen method into the training loop before sending candidates to the LLM.
+- [ ] Based on the evaluation, pick the better default for `--dedup-method` and justify the choice in MIND_MAP.md with the precision/recall numbers.
+- [ ] Run the full test suite — all tests must pass (0 failures).
+- [ ] Update MIND_MAP.md with what you built, including the evaluation results and the rationale for the default.
